<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Operates – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Town Operates</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Take basic notes on a text about a familiar topic. Reading: Summarize arguments from general and academic articles.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can take notes on town council meeting texts, recording key points on decisions and civic duties (e.g., "The city mayor stated that the Town Council is responsible for two main tasks: waste collection and road maintenance."). Can summarize arguments in articles about local governance, focusing on community involvement (e.g., "The article argues that citizens should attend town meetings to influence decisions."). Can outline civic responsibilities presented in articles, preparing for class discussions (e.g., "The importance of voting and community feedback is emphasized in the article.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a "Word Cloud" on the board or projector containing key vocabulary from Lesson 1 related to "Our Town Council" (e.g., <i>community, decisions, public services, citizens, vote, responsibilities, involvement, feedback</i>). Students are asked to, individually, write down three sentences describing what they think a town council does, using at least three words from the cloud. This activates prior knowledge and sets a reading purpose.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly skim a short paragraph to get the main idea, pointing out topic sentences. For example, the teacher might show a short text about a town council's role and demonstrate reading only the first and last sentences of each paragraph to grasp the general content. The teacher also models the pronunciation of words with the short vowel sound /ɪ/ (e.g., <i>public, civic</i>) and /e/ sound followed by "r" (e.g., <i>share, serve</i>), emphasizing clear articulation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I skimmed, did I read every single word, or just parts to get the main idea?" (Expected answer: "Just parts, for the main idea.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to find a specific date in an article, should I skim or scan?" (Expected answer: "Scan.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a printed excerpt from a simulated "Local Governance Bulletin" or a simplified local news article (B1.2 level) titled "Understanding Your Town Council: Key Responsibilities and Citizen Involvement." The text explicitly incorporates target grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals) and vocabulary (e.g., <i>decisions are made, the mayor stated that, citizens must vote, public services are provided, community feedback, participation, operate, contribute</i>). The article will detail the roles of the town council and the importance of civic duties.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand the main points of clear standard texts on familiar matters regularly encountered in work, school, leisure, etc.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article once quickly and choose the best main idea from three options provided (e.g., "a) The history of our town council, b) The functions of the town council and citizen duties, c) How to become a mayor").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again, more carefully, and decide if 5-6 statements are True or False. They must correct the False statements based on the text. (e.g., "The town council is mainly responsible for national defense." / "Community surveys are often ignored by authorities.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, identifying arguments and civic responsibilities. Focus on passive voice, reported speech, and modals ("can," "should," "must") in context. Key vocabulary: <i>decisions, civic duties, community, involvement, feedback, provide, participate, elect, responsible, operate</i>.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> The teacher provides 4-5 sentences directly from the article. Students work individually to transform them:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Change active voice to passive voice (e.g., "Citizens elect the authorities." -> "The authorities are elected by citizens.").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Change direct speech to reported speech (e.g., "The councilor said, 'We will prioritize road maintenance.'" -> "The councilor said that they would prioritize road maintenance.").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Substitute a modal verb while keeping the meaning (e.g., "Citizens should attend meetings." -> "Citizens must attend meetings to make their voice heard.").</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Note-Taking & Gap-Fill):</b> In pairs, students re-read paragraph 2 and 3 of the article. They then complete a guided note-taking template with gaps about the town council's main responsibilities and two ways citizens can get involved. The gaps will require them to extract specific words or phrases from the text, reinforcing the target vocabulary (e.g., "Public services like _______ and _______ are provided by the council." / "Citizens _______ attend meetings and _______ their feedback.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Vocabulary & Context):</b> Each pair receives a short list of 5-6 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>sustainability, participation, authorities, projects, engage</i>). They must find these words in the article, infer their meaning from context, and then collaboratively write one new sentence for each word, reflecting the theme "How Our Town Operates." They then peer-check each other's sentences for correct usage and meaning.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Summarization</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). They are given a new, short article (approximately 200-250 words, B1.2 level) titled "Community Involvement in Local Projects: The Case of the New Recycling Initiative." This article presents different arguments (pro/con or different perspectives) regarding a local project and highlights various civic responsibilities. The teacher explicitly reminds students to use formal language in civic discussions and show respect in public dialogue as they discuss the article's content.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads the article and is tasked with:
            <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Taking comprehensive notes on the key points, decisions, and specific civic duties mentioned.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Summarizing the main arguments presented in the article for community involvement or specific aspects of the initiative.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Outlining at least three civic responsibilities emphasized or implied in the text.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then prepare a brief (2-3 minute) verbal summary of their findings to share with the class. They should be encouraged to use passive voice (e.g., "Concerns were raised by citizens about..."), reported speech (e.g., "The article also stated that..."), and modals (e.g., "Citizens must participate...").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and review of group-produced notes/summaries using a checklist. Peer feedback during verbal summaries.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their verbal summaries to the class. The teacher collects the groups' written notes and summaries from the production task.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Accuracy of Notes:</b> Are key points, decisions, and civic duties accurately recorded?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarization of Arguments:</b> Are the main arguments from the article clearly and concisely summarized?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Outline of Responsibilities:</b> Are civic responsibilities correctly identified and outlined?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary & Grammar:</b> Is the core vocabulary and grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals) used appropriately in notes/summaries and verbal sharing?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute effectively?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about my civic responsibilities from today's texts is..."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to (take notes / summarize arguments / outline responsibilities) because..."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has today's reading activity improved my ability to understand and extract information from articles about local governance?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy (skimming, scanning, note-taking) did I find most useful today, and why?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the reading activities worked well for the students, particularly in summarizing arguments and outlining responsibilities?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which grammatical structures (passive voice, reported speech, modals) or vocabulary items require further reinforcement? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., graphic organizers for note-taking, sentence starters for summaries) might be needed for similar tasks in the next lesson?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information extracted and summarized from today's readings to discuss and debate specific local issues and their civic responsibilities in group discussions and role-plays, applying the vocabulary and grammar practiced today in spoken form.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">3-5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>